{"title":"Sustainable Supply Chain Management","markdown":{"headingText":"Sustainable Supply Chain Management","headingAttr":{"id":"sec-sustainability","classes":[],"keyvalue":[]},"containsRefs":false,"markdown":"\n## Why Sustainability Matters in SCM\n\nApproximately **70% of a company's carbon emissions** come from its supply chain (Scope 3 emissions under the GHG Protocol). This makes supply chain managers directly responsible for sustainability outcomes.\n\n**Regulatory drivers**:\n- EU Corporate Sustainability Reporting Directive (CSRD): Mandatory SC sustainability reporting from 2025\n- EU Supply Chain Act (CSDDD): Due diligence on human rights and environment in supply chains\n- EU ETS (Emissions Trading System): Carbon pricing increasingly internalized\n\n## The Triple Bottom Line in SCM\n\n| Dimension | Key Metrics | SCM Levers |\n|-----------|-------------|------------|\n| **Economic** | Cost, ROI, resilience | Efficiency optimization, risk management |\n| **Environmental** | CO₂e, water, waste | Mode shift, green procurement, circular design |\n| **Social** | Labor standards, safety | Supplier auditing, fair trade, EPR |\n\n## Carbon Accounting in Transport\n\n### Emission Factors by Mode\n\n```{r}\n#| label: tbl-emissions\n#| tbl-cap: \"CO₂ equivalent emission factors by transport mode\"\nlibrary(kableExtra)\n\nef <- data.frame(\n  Mode = c(\"Road truck (full)\", \"Rail (EU avg electric)\", \"Sea (container)\", \n           \"Air cargo (long haul)\", \"Inland waterway\"),\n  `CO2e (g/tkm)` = c(62, 6, 8, 500, 30),\n  Source = c(\"UBA 2023\", \"UIC 2022\", \"IMO 2022\", \"ICAO 2022\", \"EEA 2023\"),\n  check.names = FALSE\n)\nkbl(ef, booktabs = TRUE) |> kable_styling(bootstrap_options = \"striped\")\n```\n\n### Carbon Cost Calculation\n\nWith EU ETS carbon price $p_C$ (currently ~€60–80/tCO₂):\n\n$$\\text{Carbon cost} = \\text{distance} \\times \\text{volume} \\times \\text{emission factor} \\times p_C$$\n\n```{r}\n#| label: carbon-calc\n#| echo: true\n# Annual carbon cost comparison for Hamburg → Munich (800 km, 10,000 t/yr)\nroute_km <- 800\nvolume_t <- 10000\n\nmodes <- data.frame(\n  mode    = c(\"100% Road\", \"80% Rail + 20% Road\", \"100% Rail\"),\n  ef_road = c(62, 12.4, 0),   # road component (g/tkm)\n  ef_rail = c(0,  4.8,  60),  # rail component (g/tkm)\n  stringsAsFactors = FALSE\n)\n\ncarbon_price <- 70  # €/tCO2\n\nmodes$total_co2_t <- (modes$ef_road + modes$ef_rail) * route_km * volume_t / 1e9 * 1e6\n# grams to tonnes: g/tkm * km * t = g; / 1e6 = kg; / 1e3 = t\nmodes$total_co2_t <- (modes$ef_road + modes$ef_rail) * route_km * volume_t / 1e6\n\nmodes$carbon_cost_eur <- modes$total_co2_t * carbon_price\n\nprint(modes[, c(\"mode\", \"total_co2_t\", \"carbon_cost_eur\")])\n```\n\n## Circular Supply Chains\n\n### From Linear to Circular\n\n```\nLinear:   Extract → Produce → Use → Dispose\nCircular: Design for longevity → Reuse → Remanufacture → Recycle → Recover\n```\n\n**Reverse logistics** is the operational backbone of circular supply chains:\n- **Returns management**: Consumer/B2B returns processing\n- **Remanufacturing**: Restore used products to \"as-new\" condition (automotive, electronics)\n- **Recycling flows**: Material recovery\n- **Take-back programs**: EPR compliance (packaging, batteries, WEEE)\n\n### Business Case for Circularity\n\nCircular design creates value through:\n1. **Retained material value**: Remanufactured parts sell at 30–70% of new part prices but cost 40–60% less to produce\n2. **Customer loyalty**: Take-back programs build brand relationships\n3. **Regulatory compliance**: Avoid EPR fees and penalties\n4. **Resource security**: Reduce dependence on primary raw materials\n\n## Green Procurement\n\nKey elements:\n- **Environmental criteria in supplier selection**: ISO 14001 certification, carbon footprint disclosure\n- **Life Cycle Assessment (LCA)**: Evaluate environmental impact cradle-to-grave\n- **Green supplier development**: Build supplier capability vs. pure selection\n- **Total cost of ownership**: Include environmental and social costs\n\n## Exercise\n\nA manufacturer ships 50,000 tonnes of components annually from Hamburg to five European destinations. Current split: 100% road truck.\n\nProposed: Shift 60% to rail (electric) for destinations ≥ 400 km.\n\n1. Using the emission factors from the table, calculate the annual CO₂ reduction.\n2. If rail costs €0.05/tkm more than road and the average road distance is 650 km — what is the annual cost premium?\n3. At what carbon price does the modal shift break even financially?\n4. With EU ETS at €70/tCO₂, is the modal shift economically justified?\n","srcMarkdownNoYaml":""},"formats":{"html":{"identifier":{"display-name":"HTML","target-format":"html","base-format":"html"},"execute":{"fig-width":7,"fig-height":5,"fig-format":"retina","fig-dpi":96,"df-print":"default","error":false,"eval":true,"cache":null,"freeze":false,"echo":true,"output":true,"warning":false,"include":true,"keep-md":false,"keep-ipynb":false,"ipynb":null,"enabled":null,"daemon":null,"daemon-restart":false,"debug":false,"ipynb-filters":[],"ipynb-shell-interactivity":null,"plotly-connected":true,"message":false,"engine":"knitr"},"render":{"keep-tex":false,"keep-typ":false,"keep-source":false,"keep-hidden":false,"prefer-html":false,"output-divs":true,"output-ext":"html","fig-align":"center","fig-pos":null,"fig-env":null,"code-fold":true,"code-overflow":"scroll","code-link":false,"code-line-numbers":false,"code-tools":true,"tbl-colwidths":"auto","merge-includes":true,"inline-includes":false,"preserve-yaml":false,"latex-auto-mk":true,"latex-auto-install":true,"latex-clean":true,"latex-min-runs":1,"latex-max-runs":10,"latex-makeindex":"makeindex","latex-makeindex-opts":[],"latex-tlmgr-opts":[],"latex-input-paths":[],"latex-output-dir":null,"link-external-icon":false,"link-external-newwindow":false,"self-contained-math":false,"format-resources":[],"notebook-links":true},"pandoc":{"standalone":true,"wrap":"none","default-image-